                  <provenance type="#c" cert="low"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_437">Winchester,
                        Hampshire. Benedictine cathedral priory of St Peter, St Paul, and St
                        Swithun</orgName><p>This attribution was added in the Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain Supplement (1987), with the following explanation in a
                        footnote (p. 68, n. 1): 'In 1964 edn. listed under Oxford, New Coll. On fo.
                        188 is a list of books at New College in 1489 ... which is as likely to have
                        been written in Winchester as in Oxford. The book belonged to Wm. Manwode,
                        presumably the deacon of that name ord. Winchester 1484 (see Hampshire
                        Record Society vii (1892), 478, and was certainly at St Swithun's in the
                        1530s, when it was owned by Thomas Dackombe, monk and later minor
                        canon.'</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_436">Oxford,
                        Oxfordshire, New College (Collegium beate Marie Winton' in Oxonia)</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was accepted (albeit with a query) in the second edition of
                        Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). However, the attribution was
                        later rejected in the Supplement (1987) in favour of Winchester Benedictine
                        cathedral priory. The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain
                        index card for the New College attribution (which was never updated to
                        reflect the 1987 rejection) noted the list of books at the end: <q>M CCCC
                           LXXXIX hec infra scripta in studiis scolarium Wintonie Oxon. reperta sunt
                           ...</q> (compare entry for Winchester above). </p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><q>liber domini Thome
                     Dakomb</q>.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Name <q>Willelmus Manwode</q> at
                     end.</provenance>
               </history>
